# tools/al/ales_stereo_expand_env_hold_witness.rish -- stereo_expand_env_hold: the attack/hold/release downward expander carried into stereo, the linked held envelope driving one linked widening over time (ALES168).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES168.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-073427_fill-ales168-lotus-stereo-expand-env-hold.md
# Stands on: lotus/stereo_hold_env.rye (ALES165 -- the linked held follower, its stereo_env_step_hold reused whole), lotus/stereo_expand_env.rye (ALES164 -- the un-held linked expander this reduces to at a hold of zero),
# lotus/expand_env_hold.rye (ALES62 -- the mono attack/hold/release expander, its ExpandEnvHoldError reused whole and its output the left-always-peak comparison), lotus/expand.rye (ALES60 -- the deepened floor), lotus/pan.rye
# (ALES10 -- the StereoClip), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 -- the Clip, sample_max, max_clip).
#
# lotus/stereo_expand_env_hold.rye expands a span of sample-PAIRS in a master below a magnitude threshold by a ratio, the one downward widening following the shared LINKED HELD envelope over time. Each pair advances one shared
# envelope with one shared hold countdown toward the linked peak max(|left|, |right|) by ALES165's step (attack while rising and rearming the shared hold, the peak pinned while the countdown lasts, release once it expires):
# at or above the threshold both pass byte for byte (continuous at the threshold), below it the deepened floor exp = max(0, threshold − deficit-num/den) (ALES60's floor on the linked held envelope) yields one gain exp/e applied
# to both raw samples -- one detector, one hold, one widening, the image held. ExpandEnvHoldError (BadThreshold, BadRatio, BadCoeff, BadHold, BadRange) reused whole; the fusion adds no fault. Values only, never a length. The crux
# is the zero-hold law (a hold of zero reducing it to ALES164's stereo_expand_env byte for byte on both -- the held rung's no-hold limit exactly the un-held one already proven), the linked-gain-over-time law (a left-always-peak
# master with left' equal to ALES62's mono expand_env_hold(left), the right scaled by the same fraction, the independent per-channel expand_env_hold differing), the image law (the shared gain preserving the L:R ratio, an
# out-of-phase pair returning still out of phase), the knob / no-pump laws (unit ratio the identity, a nonzero hold keeping the expander idle across a dip rather than pumping), the carry law (a span in two pieces, the split
# mid-hold, equal to the whole once on both, env and hold_left carrying), and the at-threshold/silence/balance/atomicity/degenerate law.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_stereo_expand_env_hold_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-expand-env-hold: Language EN — stereo_expand_env_hold expands a span of sample-pairs in a stereo master below a magnitude threshold by a ratio, the one downward widening following the shared linked held envelope over time. Each pair advances one shared envelope with one shared hold countdown toward the linked peak (the max of the two channels' magnitudes, clamped to the rail): the attack fraction while it rises and rearms the shared hold, the peak pinned while the countdown lasts, the release only once it expires; where the held envelope sits at or above the threshold the expander is idle and both channels pass byte for byte, and where it falls below, the envelope's deficit is widened by the ratio and the single gain (the deepened floor over the envelope) is applied to both raw samples. It validates the threshold, ratio, attack, release, hold, and span once against the shared length before any write, so a refusal never expands one channel and leaves the other torn. It writes values only, never a length, so the two channels leave balanced; the caller owns the envelope and the countdown across calls, so a span expanded in two pieces equals the whole once even when the split falls mid-hold."
say "ales-stereo-expand-env-hold: Style Radiant — ALES164 carried the breathing expander into stereo: one shared envelope deepening a pair as it falls away and easing off as it rises, one widened gain applied to both channels so the image never lurches. Yet an un-held expander still deepens and re-opens on a brief dip between two loud samples — it pumps, and the ear hears the gain move across the whole field at once. A real expander carries a hold knob for exactly this: on a fall away from a peak, it pins the peak for a little while before the release begins, so the widening stays idle through the dip and the passage stays whole. ALES165 built that hold stage inside the linked follower and proved it once. This rung spends that proof: it drives ALES60's deepened floor from the linked held envelope rather than the plain linked one — the same stereo expander, now with the patience that keeps it from pumping the image. It is the stereo mirror of ALES62's held mono expander. Two proofs fused: the detector is the linked held follower (one shared envelope, one shared hold countdown pinning the linked peak across a fall so the two channels never re-open on different samples), and the gain is linked (one widened gain from the linked held envelope applied to both). The crux is six laws: the zero-hold law (a hold of zero making the linked held follower the plain linked follower, so this equals ALES164's stereo_expand_env byte for byte on both — the held rung's no-hold limit exactly the un-held rung already proven); the linked-gain-over-time law (a left-always-peak master with the left equal to ALES62's mono expand_env_hold of it byte for byte, the right scaled by the same fraction, the independent per-channel expand_env_hold genuinely differing); the image law (the shared gain preserving the left-to-right ratio, an out-of-phase pair returning still out of phase, no magnitude expanding and no sign flipping); the knob and no-pump laws (unit ratio the identity everywhere, and a nonzero hold keeping the expander idle across a brief dip — the held magnitude no smaller than the un-held one and strictly louder at the dip, the pumping avoided); the carry law (a span expanded in two pieces, the split mid-hold, equal to expanding the whole once, byte for byte on both); and the at-threshold, silence, balance, atomicity, and degenerate law (a master whose linked held envelope never falls below the threshold passing byte for byte, silence staying silence, the lengths held, each of the five refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, count zero the identity on both)."
say "ales-stereo-expand-env-hold: Lens TAME — two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) carrying one shared i64 envelope and one shared u32 hold countdown, one linked-peak comparison and at most one widen and one multiply-then-divide for the held envelope step (ALES165's proven stereo_env_step_hold) plus one per channel for the gain, all in i64 (the product of a magnitude and the deepened floor at most 32768 times 32767 never overflowing), the gain exp over e in the half-open interval zero to one so every scaled magnitude is at most its input's and fits i16; the two channels enter balanced (the StereoClip invariant asserted on entry) and leave balanced (an expander writes values only, each length asserted equal and within bound on exit, each written sign asserted held); the threshold, ratio, both coefficients, the hold, and the span are validated once before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never expands one channel and leaves the other torn; a zero held envelope is asserted to arise only when both channels are silent this pair, so there is no divide by zero, and the deepened floor is asserted within zero to the envelope so the single gain is a genuine reduction; StereoExpandEnvHoldError is ALES62's ExpandEnvHoldError (BadThreshold, BadRatio, BadCoeff, BadHold, BadRange) reused whole, the fusion adding no fault. The zero-hold law is proven by running both stereo_expand_env_hold under a hold of zero and ALES164's un-held stereo_expand_env on the same master and demanding byte-for-byte equality on both channels; the linked-gain-over-time law against a master where the left always holds the peak — the left equal to ALES62's mono expand_env_hold of it byte for byte while the right is scaled by the same fraction, the independent mono expand_env_hold of the right alone shown to genuinely differ; the image law proves no magnitude expands and no sign flips over a mixed master and that an out-of-phase pair returns still exactly out of phase; the knob and no-pump laws prove unit ratio the identity everywhere and a nonzero hold keeping the expander idle across a dip where an un-held expander pumps; the carry law proves a span expanded in two pieces, the split mid-hold, equal to the whole once on both channels; and the at-threshold, silence, balance, atomicity, and degenerate cases prove an always-loud master passing byte for byte, an all-silent master staying zero, the length held, each of the five refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, and an empty span the identity on both; siloed to lotus/, the threshold a magnitude in sample units, the ratio a plain fraction, the attack, release, and hold per sample index, no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_expand_env_hold"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_expand_env_hold.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-expand-env-hold: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_expand_env_hold.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-expand-env-hold: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-expand-env-hold: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-expand-env-hold' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-expand-env-hold: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-expand-env-hold line"
